  "endangerment": {
    "agreement": "incommensurable",
    "values": [
      {
        "value": "endangered",
        "source": "elcat-v2024.1",
        "note": "The language is well preserved in the communities of dʑa⁵³qa⁵³tu¹¹, dʐə¹¹qu¹¹, and ʂa⁴⁴pa⁵³ –– particularly so in dʑa⁵³qa⁵³tu¹¹, where only residents under the age of twenty who have attended primary school can speak Chinese. In dʐə¹¹qu¹¹ and ʂa⁴⁴pa⁵³, Namuyi residents speak Namuyi among themselves and fluent Nuosu and passable Chinese to people who prefer to speak those languages. Namuyi is less well preserved in ɕa¹¹ma¹¹kʰu⁵³, where almost all Namuyi people also speak Nuosu and Chinese: residents born before 1955 speak Namuyi to each other, but while younger people understand a great deal of Namuyi, they prefer to converse in Nuosu or Chinese. [Endangerment.Certainty: 1]",
        "scale": "elcat-v2024.1"
      },
      {
        "value": "threatened",
        "source": "elcat-v2024.1",
        "note": "[Endangerment.Certainty: 0.2]",
        "scale": "elcat-v2024.1"
      },
      {
        "value": "shifting",
        "source": "glottolog-cldf-v5.3",
        "note": "Namuyi (21-nmy) = Endangered (100 percent certain, based on the evidence available) (The language is well preserved in the communities of dʑa⁵³qa⁵³tu¹¹, dʐə¹¹qu¹¹, and ʂa⁴⁴pa⁵³ –– particularly so in dʑa⁵³qa⁵³tu¹¹, where only residents under the age of twenty who have attended primary school can speak Chinese. In dʐə¹¹qu¹¹ and ʂa⁴⁴pa⁵³, Namuyi residents speak Namuyi among themselves and fluent Nuosu and passable Chinese to people who prefer to speak those languages. Namuyi is less well preserved in ɕa¹¹ma¹¹kʰu⁵³, where almost all Namuyi people also speak Nuosu and Chinese: residents born before 1955 speak Namuyi to each other, but while younger people understand a great deal of Namuyi, they prefer to converse in Nuosu or Chinese.) [Lakhi et al. 2007](cldf:lakhi:hefright:etal:07)",
        "scale": "glottolog-cldf-v5.3"
      },
      {
        "value": "Definitely endangered",
